# {{ site_name }}

YOURLS site for <{{ site_url }}>, managed with
[yourls-manager](https://github.com/RubenFixit/yourls-manager) (`ym`) and generated from
[yourls-site-template](https://github.com/RubenFixit/yourls-site-template).

The complete site configuration lives in this repo. Secrets live only on the server.

## Layout

| Path | Owner | What |
|---|---|---|
| `site.toml` | you | The site definition: core version, plugins, deploy targets. |
| `yourls.lock.json` | `ym` | Exact core/plugin commits and tree hashes. Commit it. |
| `yourls/` | `ym` | Vendored YOURLS core + plugins. Never hand-edit; `ym status` shows drift. |
| `overlay/` | template + you | Copied over `yourls/` at build time (config shim, `health.php`, `.htaccess`, branding plugin, local plugin patches). |
| `server/config.example.php` | template | Starting point for the server-local config. |
| `tests/redirects.toml` | you | Redirect expectations checked by `ym verify`. |
| `.github/` | template | Deploy on tag, weekly update PRs, Dependabot. |
| `adopt-review/` | `ym adopt` | Only after adopting an existing install: report + files to merge by hand. Never deployed. |

Files marked *template* are updated by `ym template-update` (local edits are merged,
conflicts are marked). `site.toml`, `tests/redirects.toml` and the branding assets are never
touched by updates.

Full documentation: the
[yourls-manager README](https://github.com/RubenFixit/yourls-manager#readme).
Commands below use the globally installed `ym`
(`uv tool install git+https://github.com/RubenFixit/yourls-manager`). `uv run ym ...` runs the
version pinned in `pyproject.toml` instead, which is what CI uses.

## Setup

If this repo was created by `ym new` / `ym setup`, YOURLS core and plugins are already
vendored. (After a raw `copier copy`, run `ym core` and `ym sync` first.)

```sh
ym server-config prod                # server-local config: DB, cookie key, admin user (0600, never in Git)
ym deploy prod --dry-run             # build + show the rsync changes, change nothing
ym deploy prod
```

The server-local config lives at `<parent of web root>/yourls-config/{{ site_slug }}.php`.
The shim at `overlay/user/config.php` loads it, and `YOURLS_CONFIG_FILE` can point
elsewhere. Passwords are read from `YM_DB_PASSWORD` / `YM_ADMIN_PASSWORD` or prompted for.
To edit the file by hand, start from `server/config.example.php` (instructions inside).

First deploy to an empty database: open `{{ site_url }}/admin/install.php` once to create the
tables, then run `ym plugins prod`. Later deploys run DB upgrades and plugin activation
automatically.

Taking over a YOURLS install that already exists? Run `ym adopt --from-target prod`, review
`adopt-review/REPORT.md`, then run `ym server-config prod --from-existing` and deploy (see
the yourls-manager README).

## Day-to-day

```sh
ym status                            # versions, plugins, drift
ym check                             # what has upstream updates (exit 10 = updates)
ym deploy prod --dry-run             # build + show the rsync changes, change nothing
ym deploy prod                       # build, back up DB, rsync, upgrade, plugins, verify
ym verify prod                       # health check + tests/redirects.toml
```

### Add a plugin

1. Add a `[[plugins]]` table to `site.toml` (`name`, `repo`, optional `ref`/`path`/`active`).
2. `ym sync` vendors it into `yourls/user/plugins/<name>/` and locks the commit.
3. Commit `site.toml`, `yourls.lock.json`, `yourls/`; deploy. Deploy activates exactly the
   plugins marked `active = true`.

Remove a plugin by deleting its table and running `ym sync`. Patch a vendored plugin by
putting the changed files under `overlay/user/plugins/<name>/` (overlay wins at build time).

### Update YOURLS core and plugins

```sh
ym core --version latest             # or a specific tag; updates site.toml + lock
ym sync --update                     # plugins to their newest commits
git diff --stat                      # review
git commit -am "YOURLS X.Y.Z" && git tag vYYYY.MM.DD && git push --follow-tags
```

The `check-updates` workflow does this weekly and opens a pull request.

### Deploy from GitHub

Pushing a tag `v*` runs `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` (`ym deploy prod`) in the `production`
environment. Manual runs can pick another target. Required environment secrets:
`DEPLOY_SSH_KEY` (private key authorized on the host) and `DEPLOY_KNOWN_HOSTS`
(`ssh-keyscan -p <port> <host>`, fingerprint verified).

### Roll back

Rollback is manual and Git-based:

1. Check out the last good tag and deploy it: `git checkout v<previous> && ym deploy prod`
   (or run the deploy workflow on that tag).
2. If a DB upgrade or bad data needs undoing, restore the dump taken before the bad deploy
   from the backup dir on the server (default `<parent of web root>/yourls-backups/{{ site_slug }}/`):
   `gunzip -c <dump>.sql.gz | mysql --defaults-extra-file=<file with credentials> <db>`.

### Update the tooling

- Template files (workflows, shim, `.htaccess`, ...): `ym template-update` (needs a clean Git
  tree), then review the diff and commit. Answers are stored in `.copier-answers.yml`. Don't
  edit it by hand.
- yourls-manager pin used by CI: `ym template-update --manager-version vX.Y.Z` (bumps
  `pyproject.toml` and runs `uv lock`), then review and commit.
- Your global `ym`: `uv tool upgrade yourls-manager`.
